Rhino vendors third-party number formatting code, which KiteJS ports along with
it and which keeps its own license:

  io/github/yuroyami/kitejs/dtoa/  (MathUtils, Decimal, DoubleFormatter)
  Copyright 2018-2020 Raffaello Giulietti, MIT license. This is the Schubfach
  algorithm, the same code OpenJDK and Jackson use. Each file carries the full
  MIT text in its header.

  io/github/yuroyami/kitejs/v8dtoa/DoubleConversion
  Copyright 2011 the V8 project authors, three-clause BSD license. The file
  carries the full text in its header.

KiteJS also carries one small piece of code that did not come from Rhino, and
which keeps its own license:

  io/github/yuroyami/kitejs/WeakRef.kt and its four platform actuals
  (jvmMain, androidMain, nativeMain, jsMain), plus WeakRefTest
  Copyright 2026 yuroyami, Apache License, Version 2.0. Written for KiteCore
  (https://github.com/yuroyami/KiteCore) and copied here rather than taken as a
  dependency, because KiteCore exposes kotlinx-coroutines to everything that
  depends on it and KiteJS ships coroutines as a separate artifact. Each file
  carries the Apache boilerplate; the full text is in LICENSE-APACHE-2.0.

  Rhino uses java.util.WeakHashMap for WeakMap and WeakSet, which common Kotlin
  has no equivalent of. WeakRef is what KiteJS builds its own weak-keyed map on.

The interpreter path is ported; Rhino's JVM bytecode compiler, E4X/XML support,
Java interop (LiveConnect) and tooling are out of scope and contain no KiteJS
code.
